Cloudflare workers

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

I have the following code in a Hono middleware function that handles requests from Slack:
```
    let body
    try {
        const contentType = c.req.header('content-type');
        console.log('content-type: ', contentType);
        switch (contentType) {
            case 'application/json':
                body = await c.req.json();
                break;
            case 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded':
                body = await c.req.parseBody();
                break;
            default:
                console.error('Unexpected content-type for /slack/events: ', contentType);
                body = await c.req.text();
        }
    } catch (e) {
        console.error(e);
        throw new Error('Invalid payload')
    }
```

When slack sends a request with content type of 'application/json', it's fine.  But if slack sends 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, it fails.  

### What is the expected behavior?

the request body should be parsed.

### What do you see instead?

This is the console output:

```
content-type:  application/x-www-form-urlencoded
✘ [ERROR] TypeError: Unrecognized Content-Type header value. FormData can only parse the following MIME types: multipart/form-data, application/x-www-form-urlencoded
```

Note that the logged content-type exactly matches the expected content type in the error message. 

### Additional information

 I've also tried changing `parseBody()` to `formData()` but I get the same error.

The only thing I'm unsure about is if the actual header from Slack might be capitalized in a different way than `parseBody()` expects, but I don't know how to see the raw headers before hono parses them and lowercases them.  For my code it's find; I can see the content type; but I don't know if the underlying library that's trying to parse the form is only looking for a specific capitalization?

Hello Hono team,

I found a small gap when trying to use `validator()` to parse a dot encoded FormData. Small example:
```html
<form>
   <input name="survey_id" />
   <input name="person.firstname" />
   <input name="person.lasname" />
</form>
```


Currently `validator` doesn't support dot notation at all [validator.ts](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/b68da3945e23f0ddef8878b08df3a359eeef4a75/src/validator/validator.ts#L108)

I'm having to do this manually:
```typescript
const body = structuredClone(await c.req.parseBody({ dot: true })) ?? {};
const form =
	await MyObjZodSchema.safeParseAsync(body);
if (form.success) {
    // form.data should be { survey_id?: string; person?: { firstname?: string; lastname?: string } }
}
```

It would be nice to support c.req.valid for these cases, but I don't want to break existing form users. Either way is fine, may I suggest `formbody` or `formparse` target?

Another thing to note is that Zod isn't able to parse the result of `c.req.parseBody`, so I used `structuredClone`. I'm assuming it's due to how the object is constructed (no prototype), so it needs to be cloned before going to Zod.

